WEBVTT

NOTE
Extended audio-description track. These cues describe meaningful visuals and on-screen text not otherwise fully conveyed by the original narration. A compatible player should pause the video while longer cues are presented.

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.750
A woman with dark hair pulled into a ponytail speaks directly to the camera from a bright home-office studio. A monitor behind her displays a colorful collage.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.900
A slide titled “Lesson Objective” lists three goals with check marks: how the algorithm works for each user; how to apply that knowledge as a creator; and the main difference between TikTok and Instagram algorithms.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.200
On a dark teal title card: “What is the algorithm?” A definition appears: hundreds and thousands of algorithms operate at one time to give a user the best experience. Smaller text names the FYP, Search, Following, STEM, and Live tabs.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.700
A TikTok statement appears beside a phone image of CEO Shou Chew. One sentence is highlighted: the feed is powered by a recommendation system that delivers content likely to interest each particular user.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.850
A title card reads: “The FYP.” Behind it is a TikTok webpage about how the platform recommends videos for the For You feed.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.980
A two-step diagram titled “The FYP Explained” shows: Step 1, study what a user is interested in; then Step 2, show the user videos based on interests they will engage with.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.350
A numbered slide lists three ways TikTok determines a user’s interests: selected interests when the account was created; device and account settings; and interactions or non-actions within the app.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.750
A title card defines “interest signals” as any action or inaction a person makes in the app.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.220
The two-step FYP diagram returns with Step 1 emphasized: studying what a user is interested in.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.850
Beside a sample TikTok video, a list asks what information matters to TikTok: captions, on-screen text, sounds, hashtags, effects, trending topics, number of views, and the country or city where the video was posted.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.450
A “TikTok Search” slide compares direct Search with Recommended Search. Four phone screenshots show search results, suggested terms, a recommendation beneath a video, and a recommendation in the comments.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.100
A slide begins listing factors used to sort videos in search results: relevance and content information, engagement, user interactions, and user information such as device settings, language preferences, and location.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:09.650
A large question fills the screen: “How can I use this knowledge to my advantage as a creator?”

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:16.450
On a dark teal card: “Become a mastermind of who you want to reach.”

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:16.950
A second teal card reads: “When you think like the algorithm...”

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:32.550
A four-step slide explains how to show up in Search: identify what the audience might search for; search that phrase and study successful videos; make a video about the term; and repeat the phrase in speech, on-screen text, captions, and hashtags.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.600
A new slide asks how to work with the algorithm to show up on the FYP.

00:11:57.550 --> 00:11:59.550
Two steps appear: consider the audience’s top interests and what they watch on their FYP; then choose a video style or topic and study how others keep similar videos engaging.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:51.300
A title slide asks: “What’s the difference between TikTok’s and Instagram’s algorithm?”

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:05.650
An article titled “Powered by AI: Instagram’s Explore recommender system” appears on a desktop monitor. A highlighted excerpt says Instagram identifies similar accounts by building and inferring account embeddings.
